# --- The exploration test scenarios ---------------------------------------------------
# One title and one instruction sheet per `data/scenarios/*.tres`. The .tres carries only
# the id and the rank in the list; this is where the text lives.
msgid "SCENARIO_MOVEMENT_TITLE"
msgstr "Movement & look"

msgid "SCENARIO_MOVEMENT_DESC"
msgstr "An empty room with pillars. Watch how the look (mouse), the turns (Q/E) and the step-by-step movement (WASD) feel. Esc releases the cursor."

msgid "SCENARIO_TRAPS_TITLE"
msgstr "Traps (poison · teleport · disarray)"

msgid "SCENARIO_TRAPS_DESC"
msgstr "Step on the plates. Purple = poison (DEN drops every turn). Blue = teleport (you change tile). Orange = disarray (your next steps sometimes set off sideways)."

msgid "SCENARIO_GATES_TITLE"
msgstr "Gates (automatic · locked · meditation)"

msgid "SCENARIO_GATES_DESC"
msgstr "Gates sit BETWEEN two tiles (they are thin): you can stand on either side. Grey = opens and closes on its own as the turns pass. Golden = face it and take the “open” action (costs 2 spades). Green = face it and Meditate ×3, CONSECUTIVELY (move or turn away and the count drops back to 0). Behind the green one is a green litter: its actions only appear once the gate is open — one does not act on what one cannot see."

msgid "SCENARIO_GROUNDS_TITLE"
msgstr "Special grounds (Dig · Recycle · Examine)"

msgid "SCENARIO_GROUNDS_DESC"
msgstr "On the earthy patch: the “dig” action (spends 1 spade → an object or some information). Facing the green litter (an obstacle): the “examine” and “recycle” actions."

msgid "SCENARIO_CHESTS_TITLE"
msgstr "Chests · Crystal · Dieverting"

msgid "SCENARIO_CHESTS_DESC"
msgstr "A golden chest = loot (or a surprise teleport: nothing tells a trapped chest apart). With razél in the duo (Reveal Traps talent), a trapped chest offers “let it teleport you” or “stay put” — loot either way, more if you accept; without razél it teleports you flat, and gives nothing. A cyan crystal (an obstacle) = the “refresh” action when facing it. Orange dice = a random effect on contact: on the first you still have a spade, so the action menu offers “destroy” or “take it”; on the second (spade spent) the die rolls by itself. Six outcomes — back to the entrance or to the exit at the bottom, objects lost (left in a chest where the die was), ETH or DEN lost, rivals springing up, objects destroyed or gained."

msgid "SCENARIO_WALLS_TITLE"
msgstr "Cracked wall · Examinable decor"

msgid "SCENARIO_WALLS_DESC"
msgstr "Facing the dark wall: the “cranny crossing” action to pass through it. Facing the magenta decor: the “examine” action (encyclopaedia gain)."

msgid "SCENARIO_BRIDGE_TITLE"
msgstr "Narrow bridge (balance)"

msgid "SCENARIO_BRIDGE_DESC"
msgstr "Step onto the plank: a balance bar appears at the top. Correct CONTINUOUSLY with A/D to stay centred (do nothing, or correct too little, and a gust knocks you off). Every tile crossed costs a turn. If you fall you land at the bottom of the ravine (2 storeys, fall damage) and climb back to the start by the stairs on the right. The far end is a single walled tile, trapped with disarray: the way back is therefore made with a sloppier sideways command, and the disarray counter drops by one per bridge tile."

msgid "SCENARIO_BRIDGE_RIVAL_TITLE"
msgstr "Narrow bridge + rival"

msgid "SCENARIO_BRIDGE_RIVAL_DESC"
msgstr "The same ravine, with a rival on the OTHER side: it has to take the bridge to reach you, so you are bound to meet on a plank. It has its own balance test (a 1 to 2 % chance of falling per tile, drawn when it is created) and can therefore fall on its own on the way. If you do meet, you both fall onto the same tile and the encounter starts down below. And if it saw you fall, it may jump after you."

msgid "SCENARIO_STAIRS_TITLE"
msgstr "Stairs, elevators & falls (5 storeys)"

msgid "SCENARIO_STAIRS_DESC"
msgstr "A pyramid of five tiers. One blue staircase per landing (0→1→2→3→4), staggered towards the back: that is the way that always works. The orange platforms are SHORTCUTS doubling those stairs — one VERTICAL elevator (1↔2) and one on a COMPLEX path (3↔4) that comes out over the void and chains segments on all three axes. An elevator stays where it was left: if it stayed at the top, take the stairs. For falls: to the west each tier overhangs the next (1 unit = no damage); to the east (the last column) nothing stops you before the bottom (1/2/3/4 units = 0/10/15/20 DEN). At the bottom you can walk UNDER the tiers — and a PILLAR there holds up a corner of tier 1: its top face is directly the floor above, with no slab added. At the summit, the first two tiles of the east edge are protected by a GUARDRAIL (a low one: you see the void over it, but you do not cross it); two steps further the same edge is open, and drops you 4 storeys."

msgid "SCENARIO_ABILITIES_TITLE"
msgstr "Exploration abilities & objects"

msgid "SCENARIO_ABILITIES_DESC"
msgstr "In the action menu (Tab/Space): use “fog mantel” (the rival stops following you) or “tea drop” (cures the trap's poison)."

# --- The encounter log ---------------------------------------------------------------
# One line per thing an action or an ability effect does, as the LOG panel shows it. The
# manager prefixes each with the individual's name, so these lines start mid-sentence and
# never repeat the subject.
#
# "[to come]" marks a mechanic the engine cannot express yet: the line says what WOULD have
# happened, so the ability is visible while the system it needs is missing. See
# docs/roadmap.md.
#
# %s is a spirimonster's name unless the comment says otherwise, and ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING:
# GDScript's % takes its arguments positionally.

# The line that opens the log. TERM_ENCOUNTER is bold on its own, so the punctuation that
# follows it lives here — French puts a space before a colon, English does not.
# %s = the player characters, %s = the rivals, both comma-separated.
msgid "TERM_ENCOUNTER"
msgstr "Encounter"

msgid "LOG_ENCOUNTER_OPENING"
msgstr ": %s against %s"

# How a log line is ASSEMBLED, which is punctuation and therefore translatable: French puts
# a space before a colon and English does not. %s = the individual's name, %s = the ability
# id when there is one, %s = the line itself.
msgid "LOG_LINE"
msgstr "%s · %s"

msgid "LOG_LINE_ABILITY"
msgstr "%s · %s: %s"

# A talent's line rather than a turn's: the mark is what tells the two apart. %s = the line.
msgid "LOG_LINE_TALENT"
msgstr "⁘ %s"

